  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is G-code?",
      "a": "G-code is the list of simple instructions a 3D printer or CNC machine follows, like move to X 50, Y 20 at a set speed, or heat the nozzle to 200 degrees. Slicing software writes it from a 3D model.",
      "asks": [
        "What does a slicer do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a 3D printer move so precisely?",
      "a": "Stepper motors turn in exact steps, and belts and lead screws turn those steps into tiny straight-line movements, often around a hundredth of a millimetre per step. Counting steps gives the position.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do 3D printers use belts?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the nozzle make an object?",
      "a": "A motor pushes plastic filament into a hot end that melts it, and the nozzle squeezes out a thin line. Line by line and layer by layer, the plastic cools and fuses into the shape.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a 3D printer melt plastic?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does homing mean on a 3D printer?",
      "a": "Homing is when the printer moves each axis until it presses a small switch at the end. That sets the zero point, because stepper motors cannot sense where they are when switched on.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does my 3D printer move to the corner first?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do 3D prints sometimes shift sideways?",
      "a": "If a belt is loose or the nozzle snags, a stepper motor can skip steps. The printer carries on without knowing, so later layers print in the wrong place.",
      "asks": [
        "Why did my 3D print shift sideways?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Common 3D printer layers are about 0.1 to 0.3 millimetres thick.",
    "PLA plastic is usually printed with the nozzle at about 190 to 220 degrees Celsius."
  ],
